## Statement

Let $\mathsf E=((\mathcal X,\mathcal A),\Theta,(P_\theta)_{\theta\in\Theta})$
be a statistical experiment, let $m\ge1$, and fix a prespecified family of
nonempty null hypotheses $H_{01},\ldots,H_{0m}\subseteq\Theta$ with valid p-value
statistics $p_i:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$. For each $\theta$, put

$$I_0(\theta)=\{i:\theta\in H_{0i}\},\qquad m_0(\theta)=|I_0(\theta)|.$$

Suppose that under every $P_\theta$ the $m$ p-values are mutually independent
and that

$$P_\theta\{p_i(X)\le u\}=u\qquad(i\in I_0(\theta),\ 0\le u\le1).$$

False-null p-value laws may otherwise be arbitrary. For every $q\in(0,1)$,
the BH rule in [[alg-benjamini-hochberg-step-up-procedure]] satisfies

$$\operatorname{FDR}_\theta=\frac{m_0(\theta)}{m}q\le q.$$

This is an FDR guarantee; no FWER bound is asserted.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The fixed finite family and statistical experiment in the
Statement, $q\in(0,1)$, and the stated independence and exact-uniformity
conditions under each $P_\theta$.

[F1] [[def-false-discovery-rate-and-familywise-error-rate]]: for true-null
index set $I_0$, $V$ counts rejected true nulls, $R$ is the number rejected,
and $\operatorname{FDR}=\mathbb E[V/(R\vee1)]$.

[F2] [[alg-benjamini-hochberg-step-up-procedure]]: for sorted values
$p_{(k)}$, BH takes the largest $R$ with $p_{(R)}\le Rq/m$ and rejects the
first $R$ ranks.

[F3] [[def-p-value]]: a p-value statistic is a measurable statistic into
$[0,1]$; validity requires $P_\theta(p_i\le u)\le u$ under every null law.

[F4] [[def-independent-random-elements]]: random elements on one probability
space are independent when their generated sigma-algebras are independent.

[F5] [[thm-grouping-independent-sigma-algebras]]: disjoint groups of an
independent family of sigma-algebras remain independent after taking the
sigma-algebras they generate.

[F6] [[thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law]]: a finite
independent family of random elements has joint law equal to the finite
product of its marginal laws.

[F7] [[lem-benjamini-hochberg-leave-one-out-identity]]: for each $i$, the
leave-one-out variable $S_i$ is measurable with respect to the other
p-values, and $I_i/(R\vee1)=\mathbf 1\{p_i\le(S_i+1)q/m\}/(S_i+1)$.

[F8] [[cor-expectation-of-an-indicator-is-probability]]: for every event $A$,
$\mathbb E[\mathbf 1_A]=P(A)$.

[F9] [[cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound]]: finite sums
of integrable random variables have expectation equal to the sum of their
expectations.

[F10] [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]:
expectation is the integral against the specified probability law.

[A1] For every fixed $\theta$, the p-values are mutually independent under
$P_\theta$, and each $i\in I_0(\theta)$ has the exact uniform law, so
$P_\theta(p_i\le u)=u$ on $[0,1]$.

## Proof

1.1 Fix $\theta$ and $i\in I_0(\theta)$. By [F3], each p-value is a measurable $[0,1]$-valued random element; [A1] and [F4] give independence, [F5] groups $p_i$ and the remaining p-values, and [F7] makes $S_i$ measurable with respect to the latter group. Let $R$ and the rejection indicators be those returned by [F2]; then $p_i$ and $S_i$ are independent random elements, and [F6] gives their product joint law. [given, A1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, choose]

2.1 For $r\in\{0,\ldots,m-1\}$ put $t_r=(r+1)q/m\le q<1$. By [A1], $P_\theta(p_i\le t_r)=t_r$; independence from step 1.1 and the product law [F6] give $P_\theta(p_i\le t_r,\ S_i=r)=t_rP_\theta(S_i=r)$. [A1, F6, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 By [F7], partitioning on the finite-valued $S_i$ expresses $I_i/(R\vee1)$ as $\sum_{r=0}^{m-1}\mathbf 1\{p_i\le t_r,S_i=r\}/(r+1)$. Each summand is measurable and bounded, hence integrable under [F10]; [F8], [F9], and step 2.1 then give $\mathbb E_\theta[I_i/(R\vee1)]=\sum_{r=0}^{m-1}t_rP_\theta(S_i=r)/(r+1)=(q/m)\sum_{r=0}^{m-1}P_\theta(S_i=r)=q/m$. [F7, F8, F9, F10, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 By [F1], $V=\sum_{i\in I_0(\theta)}I_i$ and $\operatorname{FDR}_\theta=\mathbb E_\theta[V/(R\vee1)]$; repeated finite linearity [F9] and step 3.1 give $\operatorname{FDR}_\theta=\sum_{i\in I_0(\theta)}q/m=(m_0(\theta)/m)q\le q$, since $m_0(\theta)\le m$. This is the FDR defined in [F1], not an FWER conclusion. [F1, F9, step 3.1, algebra] ∎

## Remarks

No choice axiom is used: the family is finite, each leave-one-out index is
fixed in the proof, and the product-law supplier is applied only to finite
families.
